diagnosis_text:
The Location C router (router4) is up and routing normally. From PC11 (192.168.200.11) the default gateway 192.168.200.1 responds to ICMP (sub-millisecond RTTs), confirming L2/L3 reachability on the local VLAN. Inter-site connectivity is also working: PC11 successfully pinged remote hosts/subnets (e.g., 172.16.10.4 and 172.16.10.5), and could reach router4’s upstream neighbors 192.168.15.1 and 172.25.1.2. On router4, interfaces are up for the links in use (eth0: 192.168.200.1/24, eth4: 192.168.15.2/24, eth5: 172.25.1.1/24), RIP is running and advertising/learning routes, and the routing table contains a valid default route via 172.25.1.2 plus multiple remote prefixes learned via RIP. UDP/520 is listening, indicating RIP is active, and the kernel FIB matches FRR’s selected routes.

Mechanism: If router4 were down, ARP to 192.168.200.1 would fail and PC11 wouldn’t reach remote subnets. Instead, ARP and ICMP succeed and remote routes are present, so the observed “cannot get out” symptom is not occurring now.

Localization: Router4 (eth0/eth4/eth5) and the L2 path via switch4/5/6 are operational; PC11-to-gateway works; upstream neighbors are reachable.

Root cause: No network fault found; the earlier outage may have been transient or host-specific.

Corrective fix: No changes required on the network. If users still experience issues, check individual hosts (IP/DNS settings, firewall) or transient switch port/PC issues. If the problem recurs, collect timestamps and affected hosts to correlate with device logs on router4 and access switches. === END ===